AEM Authentication — Connector Requirements
5 Oct 2026 · @Refold CS
What a custom connector needs in order to authenticate against Adobe Experience Manager as a Cloud Service.
Overview
This workflow authenticates to AEM using Adobe OAuth Server-to-Server (the client_credentials grant). It does not use the retired Service Account (JWT) flow.
The flow is straight-line, with no signing and no refresh step:
1. POST the four credential fields to the token endpoint.
2. Read access_token from the response.
3. Send it as Authorization: Bearer <token> on every AEM request.
Requests target the AEM author tier. Every value below is environment-specific and set by the operator.
Endpoints

Pattern
Set by
Token endpoint
https://ims-<region>.adobelogin.com/ims/token/v3
Adobe; region is fixed per org
AEM base URL
https://author-p<program>-e<environment>.adobeaemcloud.com
Operator, per environment
Both are configuration, not secrets. Store the base URL as a complete host so request paths can be appended directly.
Request fields
Four fields, all required, sent as application/x-www-form-urlencoded in the POST body:
Field
Required
Value
grant_type
Yes
Constant: client_credentials
client_id
Yes
From the Developer Console credential
client_secret
Yes
From the same credential — secret, never committed
scope
Yes
Comma-separated list from the same credential; must include the AEM content-fragment management scope, or calls return 403 with a valid token
Nothing else is needed. No private key, no technicalAccountId, no orgId — those belong to the retired JWT flow.
Sample request
POST /ims/token/v3 HTTP/1.1
Host: ims-<region>.adobelogin.com
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=client_credentials&client_id=<client_id>&client_secret=<client_secret>&scope=<scope_1>%2C<scope_2>%2C<scope_3>
As curl:
curl -X POST "https://ims-<region>.adobelogin.com/ims/token/v3" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded" \
  --data-urlencode "grant_type=client_credentials" \
  --data-urlencode "client_id=<client_id>" \
  --data-urlencode "client_secret=<client_secret>" \
  --data-urlencode "scope=<comma-separated scopes>"
The scope list is one field holding comma-separated values, not a repeated field. Send no Authorization header on this call — the credentials travel in the body.
Response fields
Field
Use
access_token
