token_type
bearer
expires_in
Lifetime in seconds (~86400). Cache the token and re-request before it expires.
Sample response
{
  "access_token": "eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIsIng1dSI6...",
  "token_type": "bearer",
  "expires_in": 86399
}
On failure IMS returns HTTP 400 with error and error_description. A wrong secret gives invalid_client; a scope the credential does not hold gives invalid_scope.
Token lifecycle
There is no refresh token. The client credentials grant has no refresh step — to renew, re-send the same request body to the same endpoint.
Refresh rules for the connector:
• Cache the token keyed on client_id + scope, with the absolute expiry computed at fetch time (now + expires_in).
• Renew early. Treat the token as expired a margin before expires_in — 5 minutes is a safe default — so a long-running request never crosses the boundary mid-flight.
• Single-flight. When several calls find the cache empty at once, let one fetch and have the rest wait on it. Parallel workers otherwise stampede the token endpoint.
• Renew on 401, once. A 401 from AEM means the token was rejected: discard the cached token, fetch a new one, retry the request once. A second 401 is a credential problem, not an expiry problem — surface it.
• Do not retry a 403. That is an authorization failure, not an auth failure: the token is valid but the credential lacks the scope or the product profile. Retrying will not fix it.
Tokens are bearer credentials — treat a cached token with the same care as the secret. Keep it in memory, never on disk or in logs.
Using the token
Every AEM request carries the token in a single header:
Authorization: Bearer <access_token>
Nothing else about the request changes. AEM validates the token against IMS, resolves the technical account to a repository principal, and applies that principal's permissions — so a valid token with insufficient product-profile access still returns 403.
